mmc: txs02612: do not register hosts the driver cannot serve

txs_probe() looks up the MMC host it multiplexes, reports plainly when it
cannot find one, and then registers two card hosts anyway because the
test that should have stopped it is written "if (1 || ...)".

On the Pyra the device tree keeps a stub txs02612 node at the root with
no mmc phandle, so this happens on every boot.  Two phantom hosts appear,
the MMC core tries to reset a card on each, and those requests never
complete; the rescan workers sit in uninterruptible sleep from boot
onwards.  That costs no CPU, but it breaks suspend outright:
mmc_pm_notify() cancels each host's card-detect work and waits for it, so
the system never gets as far as freezing tasks, and systemd never thaws
the user session it froze on the way in.  The desktop is left frozen with
a working mouse pointer and the only recovery is "systemctl thaw
user.slice".

Drop the forced condition.  The sysfs "switch" control is created earlier
in the same function, so manual muxing of the two card slots still works.

--- a/drivers/mmc/host/txs02612.c	2026-09-10 22:19:00.604520246 -0400
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/txs02612.c	2026-09-10 22:19:19.914919745 -0400
@@ -292,7 +292,7 @@
 
 	mutex_init(&data->lock);
 
-	if (1 || !IS_ERR_OR_NULL(data->host)) {
+	if (!IS_ERR_OR_NULL(data->host)) {
 		/* setup our client ports */
 
 		// alternatively loop over OF children
